  "name": "The big picture",
  "slides": [
    {
      "title": "The whole machine on one screen",
      "summary": "An LLM reads some text and guesses what comes next. That is all it does.",
      "body": [
        "So far we treated the model as one closed box. Now we open it.",
        "An LLM does one simple job: it reads some text and guesses the next word.",
        "Give it “The cat sat on the”. It gives every possible next word a score: “mat” scores high, “moon” scores low.",
        "<mark>To write a full answer, it repeats that job: guess one word, add it to the text, guess again.</mark> The next slide shows this loop.",
        {
          "h": "How to read the shapes"
        },
        "Each stage on the right turns one block of numbers into another. A block of numbers with several dimensions is called a <b>tensor</b>, and its <b>shape</b> lists how big each dimension is. Following the shapes is the quickest way to understand how the model works. Four letters come up everywhere:",
        {
          "ul": [
            "<code>B</code>, batch size: how many separate texts are handled at once.",
            "<code>n</code>, sequence length: how many tokens are in each text.",
            "<code>d</code>, model width: how many numbers describe each token. Usually 4,096 or more.",
            "<code>V</code>, vocabulary size: how many different tokens the model knows. Often 100,000 or more."
          ]
        },
        "Click a stage on the right to jump to its chapter."
      ],
      "viz": [
        {
          "type": "stages",
          "items": [
            {
              "label": "Text",
              "value": "string",
              "goto": {
                "chapter": "tokens"
              }
            },
            {
              "label": "Tokenizer",
              "value": "[B, n] int ids",
              "goto": {
                "chapter": "tokens"
              }
            },
            {
              "label": "Embeddings + position",
              "value": "[B, n, d]",
              "goto": {
                "chapter": "embeddings"
              }
            },
            {
              "label": "Transformer block × N",
              "value": "[B, n, d] → [B, n, d]",
              "goto": {
                "chapter": "attention"
              }
            },
            {
              "label": "  · attention",
              "value": "mixes across tokens",
              "goto": {
                "chapter": "attention"
              }
            },
            {
              "label": "  · MLP",
              "value": "thinks per token",
              "goto": {
                "chapter": "block"
              }
            },
            {
              "label": "Final norm + unembed",
              "value": "[B, n, V] logits",
              "goto": {
                "chapter": "output"
              }
            },
            {
              "label": "Softmax + sampling",
              "value": "1 next token id",
              "goto": {
                "chapter": "output"
              }
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "caption",
          "html": "Typical sizes: d = 4,096, N = 32 layers, V = 128,000. When generating, only the scores for the last token matter."
        }
      ],
      "figure": {
        "src": "images/forward-pass-light.svg",
        "darkSrc": "images/forward-pass-dark.svg",
        "alt": "Text is split into token IDs, each ID becomes a vector of d numbers, the vectors flow through N transformer blocks, and the final layer gives a score for every vocabulary token. Softmax turns the last position's scores into probabilities, one token is picked, appended to the text, and the whole pass runs again.",
        "width": 1200,
        "height": 560,
        "caption": "The whole model on one page. Follow the shapes: <code>[n]</code> → <code>[n, d]</code> → <code>[n, V]</code>. Numbers are illustrative."
      }
    },
    {
      "title": "Generation is a loop",
      "summary": "The model only ever predicts one token. Long answers come from running it in a loop and feeding each prediction back in.",
      "body": [
        "The model can't write a sentence in one go. It predicts just the next token. The program adds that token to the input, and the model runs again on the longer text. This is called <b>autoregressive decoding</b>: each output becomes part of the next input.",
        "Press <i>Run one step</i> to watch it happen. Each step reads everything so far.",
        {
          "h": "Two stages, with very different costs"
        },
        {
          "ul": [
            "<b>Prefill</b>: first the whole prompt is processed, all its tokens at the same time. GPUs are great at doing lots of maths at once, so the limit here is pure calculation speed (<b>compute-bound</b>). It's the short pause before the first word appears.",
            "<b>Decode</b>: after that, every new token needs its own run through the whole model. Each run does little maths but has to read billions of weights from GPU memory, so the limit is how fast memory can be read (<b>memory-bound</b>). It's the steady word-by-word typing you see. The KV cache (see <i>Output & inference</i>) exists to make this stage cheaper.",
            "<b>Stopping</b>: the loop ends when the model writes a special \"end\" token, or when the app's length limit is reached."
          ]
        },
        {
          "note": "<mark>Everything an LLM writes, including code, step-by-step reasoning and tool calls, comes out of this loop one token at a time.</mark>",
          "kind": "key"
        }
      ],
